      framework_description: "Understanding human behavior requires rigorous scientific methods. Researchers use systematic approaches to observe, measure, and analyze behavior while minimizing bias and error.\n.1. Experimental Methods\nThe gold standard for establishing cause-and-effect relationships. Participants are randomly assigned to conditions, the independent variable is manipulated, and effects on the dependent variable are measured. Random assignment helps control for confounding variables.\n.2. Correlational Studies\nWhen experiments are impractical or unethical, researchers examine relationships between variables. Correlation coefficients range from -1 to +1. Important: correlation does not imply causation. A third variable may explain the relationship.\n.3. Observational Research\nNaturalistic observation provides rich data about behavior in real-world settings. Observer bias and reactivity (the Hawthorne effect) are key limitations. Structured observations use predefined coding systems for reliability.",
