      title: "Classical Conditioning: Pavlov's Legacy",
      framework_description: "Classical conditioning is a fundamental learning process discovered by Ivan Pavlov in the 1890s. It explains how neutral stimuli can acquire the ability to trigger automatic responses through association.\n.1. Pavlov's Original Experiment\nPavlov noticed that dogs salivated not only when food touched their tongues but also at the sight of the lab assistant who fed them. He systematically paired a bell (neutral stimulus) with food (unconditioned stimulus). After repeated pairings, the bell alone triggered salivation.\n.2. Acquisition and Extinction\nAcquisition is the initial learning phase where the conditioned stimulus is repeatedly paired with the unconditioned stimulus. Timing matters — the CS typically precedes the US by less than a second for optimal learning. Extinction occurs when the CS is repeatedly presented without the US, gradually reducing the conditioned response.\n.3. Real-World Applications\nClassical conditioning explains many everyday phenomena: phobias (fear paired with neutral stimuli), taste aversions (sickness paired with food), advertising (products paired with positive emotions), and even some medical treatments like aversion therapy for addiction.",
