        { heading: "Modern Neural Understanding", source: "Kandel, E.R. (2001)", body: "<p>Neuroscience has revealed the neural basis of classical conditioning. The cerebellum plays a key role in simple conditioned reflexes, while the amygdala is central to conditioned fear responses. Long-term potentiation (LTP) strengthens synaptic connections between neurons that fire together.</p><p>This neural plasticity is the biological substrate of learning. Understanding these mechanisms has led to pharmacological treatments that enhance or inhibit fear conditioning, with implications for PTSD and anxiety disorders.</p>" , evidence: "Empirical — neuroscience studies identify conditioning circuits in the brain."}
