      id: "concept-operant",
      title: "Operant Conditioning: Skinner's Behaviorism",
      framework_description: "Operant conditioning, developed by B.F. Skinner, explains how voluntary behaviors are shaped by their consequences. Unlike classical conditioning (which focuses on involuntary responses), operant conditioning deals with how organisms learn to act on their environment.\n.1. The Law of Effect\nEdward Thorndike's Law of Effect states that behaviors followed by satisfying consequences are more likely to be repeated, while behaviors followed by unpleasant consequences are less likely to recur. This simple principle is the foundation of operant conditioning.\n.2. Reinforcement Schedules\nPartial reinforcement schedules produce different patterns of behavior. Fixed-ratio schedules produce high response rates, variable-ratio schedules produce steady, persistent responding, fixed-interval schedules produce a scalloped pattern, and variable-interval schedules produce moderate, steady responding. Variable schedules are most resistant to extinction.\n.3. Applications and Critiques\nOperant conditioning principles are widely used in education (token economies), parenting (reward systems), clinical psychology (behavior modification), and animal training. Critics argue that it oversimplifies human behavior and neglects cognition, emotion, and intrinsic motivation.",
