      framework_description: "Social influence research examines how the real or imagined presence of others shapes individual behavior. Three key phenomena — conformity, compliance, and obedience — represent different forms of social influence.\n.1. Asch's Conformity Experiments\nSolomon Asch (1951) demonstrated that people conform to group pressure even when the group is clearly wrong. In his line judgment task, 75% of participants conformed at least once. Conformity increased with group size up to 4-5 people, then plateaued. The presence of a single dissenter dramatically reduced conformity.\n.2. Milgram's Obedience Studies\nStanley Milgram (1963) investigated obedience to authority. Participants were told to administer electric shocks to a learner. 65% delivered the maximum 450-volt shock. Key factors: proximity to authority, proximity to victim, and gradual escalation. The experiments raised profound ethical questions about human nature.\n.3. Cialdini's Principles of Influence\nRobert Cialdini identified six universal principles: reciprocity (give and take), scarcity (limited availability), authority (expert influence), consistency (commitment), liking (similarity and familiarity), and social proof (following others).",
