    {
        id: "phase7a-mindset_dive1",
        title: "Fixed vs. Growth Mindset: How Beliefs About Ability Shape Challenge, Effort, and Failure",
        framework_description: "Carol Dweck's Mindset makes a deceptively simple claim: what you believe about intelligence and talent shapes a surprisingly large part of how you live. People with the fixed mindset believe their basic qualities are carved in stone, so every situation becomes a test of whether they look smart or dumb, accepted or rejected, a winner or a loser. People with the growth mindset believe their basic qualities can be cultivated through effort, strategies, and help from others, so the hand they were dealt is only the starting point for development. This dive covers the two mindsets, the praise experiments that show how the beliefs get transmitted to children, and the false growth mindset that emerged once the term became a buzzword.\n.1. Two Mindsets, Two Meaning Systems\nIn the fixed mindset the goal is to prove yourself at every turn, because your traits are fixed quantities and risk and effort might reveal that you do not have enough of them. In the growth mindset the goal is to grow, and failure is not a verdict on your identity but information that tells you what to try next. Dweck watched children react to hard puzzles: some wanted nothing to do with them, while others rubbed their hands together and shouted that they loved a challenge, because they did not think they were failing, they thought they were learning. The two mindsets produce opposite ideas about challenge, effort, and failure from the very same starting point, and Dweck is explicit that nobody is purely one or the other; everyone is a mixture, and different situations trigger different mindsets.\n.2. The Praise Experiments\nTo test how beliefs are transmitted, Dweck's lab ran studies with hundreds of students, mostly early adolescents, who solved fairly difficult problems from a nonverbal IQ test and were then praised in one of two ways. Some students were told they must be smart at this, others that they must have worked really hard, and although the groups were exactly equal to begin with, they split sharply right after. Ninety percent of the effort-praised students chose a challenging new task they could learn from, while the ability-praised students rejected it, their performance plummeted once the problems got hard, and nearly forty percent of them lied about their scores so their imperfections would not show. Same test, same scores, same children; only the belief handed to them by the praise differed, and a later study found that parents' process praise to one- to three-year-olds predicted the motivational frameworks those children held five years later.\n.3. The False Growth Mindset\nDweck added a section on the false growth mindset after her colleague Susan Mackie reported an outbreak of it, people claiming to have a growth mindset who, on closer inspection, did not. It shows up as mistaking open-mindedness for growth, reducing growth to effort alone, praising effort that is not actually there, and using effort praise as a consolation prize when children are not learning; Dweck is blunt that her praise research always tied the praised process to real learning, progress, and achievement. She also warns against the empty reassurance of telling children they can do anything without giving them the skills and resources to get there, and against all-or-nothing self-labeling, since every person is a mixture of both mindsets. The measured caveat is that meta-analyses of mindset interventions find small and uneven effects, and the largest national trial co-authored by Dweck found modest average gains concentrated among lower-achieving students, so the honest conclusion is that beliefs matter, not that a single slogan transforms achievement.",
        svg: "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 820 300\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"820\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#181825\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"410\" y=\"42\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"24\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#f5f7fa\">Fixed vs. Growth Mindset</text><text x=\"410\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#8f96a8\">Two beliefs about ability, three visible reactions</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"90\" width=\"364\" height=\"168\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#33263a\"/><rect x=\"422\" y=\"90\" width=\"364\" height=\"168\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#1e3a30\"/><text x=\"216\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">FIXED MINDSET</text><text x=\"604\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">GROWTH MINDSET</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"152\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#d9cfe0\">Challenge feels risky to expose</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"182\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#d9cfe0\">Effort signals low ability</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"212\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#d9cfe0\">Failure reads as a verdict</text><text x=\"440\" y=\"152\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#cfe0d8\">Challenge is a chance to stretch</text><text x=\"440\" y=\"182\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#cfe0d8\">Effort is how ability grows</text><text x=\"440\" y=\"212\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#cfe0d8\">Failure becomes data to learn from</text><text x=\"410\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#8f96a8\">Dweck: everyone is a mixture of both mindsets</text></svg>",
        key_takeaways: [
          "A mindset is a belief about the malleability of your basic qualities: the fixed mindset treats intelligence and talent as fixed quantities to be proven, the growth mindset as qualities that can be cultivated through effort, strategies, and help from others.",
          "In Dweck's praise studies, ability praise pushed children into the fixed mindset: they rejected challenges, their performance plummeted after difficulty, and nearly forty percent lied about their scores, while effort praise led ninety percent of children to choose a challenge and to keep performing well.",
          "The mindsets announce themselves in reactions to challenge, effort, and failure: the fixed mindset avoids what might expose deficiency and reads failure as a verdict, the growth mindset seeks stretch and treats failure as information.",
          "The false growth mindset mistranslates the idea into slogans, effort praise without learning, and you-can-do-anything reassurance, and all-or-nothing self-labeling misses Dweck's point that everyone is a mixture of both mindsets.",
        ],
        sections: [
          { heading: "The Praise Experiment That Changed How Adults Talk to Children", source: "Dweck, C. S. (2006)", body: "In the studies that made praise famous, hundreds of students, mostly early adolescents, solved the same fairly difficult nonverbal IQ problems and then received one of two messages: praise for ability, such as you must be smart at this, or praise for effort, such as you must have worked really hard. The groups started out exactly equal, yet afterwards ninety percent of the effort-praised students chose a challenging new task they could learn from, while the ability-praised students avoided it, performed worse once the problems became hard, and nearly forty percent lied about their scores. Later meta-analyses found smaller and uneven effects for mindset interventions, and the largest national trial co-authored by Dweck found modest average gains concentrated among lower-achieving students, which is the honest nuance to keep alongside the original findings." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — the praise studies are influential but replication attempts have been mixed."},
          { heading: "The False Growth Mindset and the Buzzword Problem", source: "Dweck, C. S. (2016)", body: "After her Australian colleague Susan Mackie told her about an outbreak of a false growth mindset, Dweck added a section to the updated edition cataloguing how the idea was being misunderstood: open-mindedness was being mistaken for growth, growth was being reduced to effort alone, effort that was not there was being praised, and effort praise was being used as a consolation prize for children who were not learning. Her correction is that the process praised in her research was always tied to learning, progress, and achievement, that telling children they can do anything is empty reassurance without the skills and resources to back it up, and that everyone is a mixture of both mindsets, so labeling yourself all or nothing misses the point." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — author's commentary on popularization; the underlying meta-analytic support is weak."},
        ],
        sv: {
          title: "Fixerat och utvecklande tankes\u00e4tt: vad Dwecks forskning faktiskt visar",
          framework_description: "Carol Dwecks Mindset bygger p\u00e5 en f\u00f6rv\u00e5nansv\u00e4rt enkel tes: det du tror om intelligens och talang formar en f\u00f6rv\u00e5nansv\u00e4rt stor del av hur du lever. Den som har ett fixerat tankes\u00e4tt tror att de grundl\u00e4ggande egenskaperna \u00e4r huggna i sten, och varje situation blir d\u00e4rf\u00f6r ett prov p\u00e5 om man ser smart eller dum ut, blir accepterad eller avvisad, \u00e4r en vinnare eller en f\u00f6rlorare. Den som har ett utvecklande tankes\u00e4tt tror att grundl\u00e4ggande f\u00f6rm\u00e5gor kan odlas genom anstr\u00e4ngning, strategier och hj\u00e4lp fr\u00e5n andra, och att handen man f\u00e5tt bara \u00e4r utg\u00e5ngspunkten f\u00f6r utveckling. Den h\u00e4r guiden g\u00e5r igenom de tv\u00e5 tankes\u00e4tten, ber\u00f6mstudierna som visar hur \u00f6vertygelserna f\u00f6rs vidare till barn och det falska utvecklande tankes\u00e4ttet som uppstod n\u00e4r ordet blev en modefluga.\n.1. Tv\u00e5 tankes\u00e4tt, tv\u00e5 system av betydelser\nI det fixerade tankes\u00e4ttet \u00e4r m\u00e5let att bevisa sig sj\u00e4lv vid varje tillf\u00e4lle, eftersom ens egenskaper \u00e4r fasta kvantiteter och risk och anstr\u00e4ngning kan avsl\u00f6ja att man inte har tillr\u00e4ckligt av dem. I det utvecklande tankes\u00e4ttet \u00e4r m\u00e5let att v\u00e4xa, och ett misslyckande \u00e4r inte en dom \u00f6ver ens identitet utan information om vad man ska pr\u00f6va h\u00e4rn\u00e4st. Dweck s\u00e5g hur barn reagerade p\u00e5 sv\u00e5ra pussel: en del ville inte r\u00f6ra dem, medan andra gned h\u00e4nderna och ropade att de \u00e4lskade en utmaning, f\u00f6r de tyckte inte att de misslyckades, de tyckte att de l\u00e4rde sig. De tv\u00e5 tankes\u00e4tten ger motsatta id\u00e9er om utmaning, anstr\u00e4ngning och misslyckande fr\u00e5n exakt samma utg\u00e5ngspunkt, och Dweck \u00e4r tydlig med att ingen \u00e4r rent det ena eller det andra; alla \u00e4r en blandning och olika situationer v\u00e4cker olika tankes\u00e4tt.\n.2. Ber\u00f6mstudierna\nF\u00f6r att testa hur \u00f6vertygelser f\u00f6rs vidare genomf\u00f6rde Dwecks labb studier med hundratals elever, f\u00f6r det mesta tidiga ton\u00e5ringar, som l\u00f6ste ganska sv\u00e5ra uppgifter fr\u00e5n ett icke-verbalt IQ-test och sedan fick ber\u00f6m p\u00e5 ett av tv\u00e5 s\u00e4tt. En del fick h\u00f6ra att de m\u00e5ste vara smarta p\u00e5 detta, andra att de m\u00e5ste ha jobbat v\u00e4ldigt h\u00e5rt, och fast\u00e4n grupperna var exakt lika fr\u00e5n b\u00f6rjan gick de tv\u00e4rt is\u00e4r direkt efter\u00e5t. Nittio procent av de som ber\u00f6mts f\u00f6r anstr\u00e4ngning valde en utmanande ny uppgift att l\u00e4ra sig av, medan de som ber\u00f6mts f\u00f6r f\u00f6rm\u00e5ga avvisade den, presterade betydligt s\u00e4mre n\u00e4r uppgifterna blev sv\u00e5ra och n\u00e4stan fyrtio procent av dem lj\u00f6g om sina resultat f\u00f6r att deras brister inte skulle synas. Samma test, samma resultat, samma barn; enda skillnaden var den \u00f6vertygelse ber\u00f6mmet gav dem, och en senare studie visade att f\u00f6r\u00e4ldrars processber\u00f6m till sm\u00e5barn i \u00e5ldern ett till tre \u00e5r f\u00f6rutsp\u00e5dde vilket motivationsm\u00f6nster barnen hade fem \u00e5r senare.\n.3. Det falska utvecklande tankes\u00e4ttet\nDweck lade till ett avsnitt om det falska utvecklande tankes\u00e4ttet efter att hennes kollega Susan Mackie rapporterat om ett utbrott av det: m\u00e4nniskor som h\u00e4vdade att de hade ett utvecklande tankes\u00e4tt men som vid n\u00e4rmare granskning inte alls hade det. Det visar sig som att ta \u00f6ppenhet f\u00f6r tillv\u00e4xt, att reducera tillv\u00e4xt till ren anstr\u00e4ngning, att ber\u00f6mma anstr\u00e4ngning som inte finns och att anv\u00e4nda anstr\u00e4ngningsber\u00f6m som tr\u00f6stpris n\u00e4r barn inte l\u00e4r sig; Dweck \u00e4r rak med att hennes ber\u00f6mforskning alltid kn\u00f6t processen de ber\u00f6mde till faktiskt l\u00e4rande, framsteg och prestation. Hon varnar ocks\u00e5 f\u00f6r den tomma uppmuntran det inneb\u00e4r att s\u00e4ga till barn att de kan allt utan att ge dem f\u00e4rdigheter och resurser att ta sig dit, och f\u00f6r allt-eller-inget-st\u00e4mplar, eftersom varje m\u00e4nniska \u00e4r en blandning av b\u00e5da tankes\u00e4tten. En \u00e4rlig avv\u00e4gning \u00e4r att metaanalyser av tankes\u00e4ttsinterventioner ger sm\u00e5 och oj\u00e4mna effekter, och att den st\u00f6rsta nationella studien med Dweck som medf\u00f6rfattare visade blygsamma genomsnittliga vinster som fr\u00e4mst fanns hos elever med svagare prestationer, s\u00e5 slutsatsen \u00e4r att \u00f6vertygelser spelar roll, inte att en enda slogan f\u00f6rvandlar prestationer.",
          key_takeaways: [
            "Ett tankes\u00e4tt \u00e4r en \u00f6vertygelse om hur formbara dina grundl\u00e4ggande egenskaper \u00e4r: det fixerade tankes\u00e4ttet ser intelligens och talang som fasta kvantiteter att bevisa, det utvecklande som f\u00f6rm\u00e5gor som kan odlas genom anstr\u00e4ngning, strategier och hj\u00e4lp fr\u00e5n andra.",
            "I Dwecks ber\u00f6mstudier pressade ber\u00f6m f\u00f6r f\u00f6rm\u00e5ga barnen in i det fixerade tankes\u00e4ttet: de avvisade utmaningar, presterade s\u00e4mre efter sv\u00e5righeter och n\u00e4stan fyrtio procent lj\u00f6g om sina resultat, medan ber\u00f6m f\u00f6r anstr\u00e4ngning fick nittio procent att v\u00e4lja en utmaning och forts\u00e4tta prestera v\u00e4l.",
            "Tankes\u00e4tten avsl\u00f6jar sig i reaktionerna p\u00e5 utmaning, anstr\u00e4ngning och misslyckande: det fixerade tankes\u00e4ttet undviker det som kan blotta brister och ser misslyckande som en dom, det utvecklande s\u00f6ker utmaningar och ser misslyckande som information.",
            "Det falska utvecklande tankes\u00e4ttet f\u00f6rvandlar id\u00e9n till slogans, anstr\u00e4ngningsber\u00f6m utan l\u00e4rande och du-kan-allt-uppmuntran, och allt-eller-inget-st\u00e4mplar missar Dwecks po\u00e4ng att alla \u00e4r en blandning av b\u00e5da tankes\u00e4tten.",
          ],
          sections: [
            { heading: "Ber\u00f6mstudien som \u00e4ndrade hur vuxna talar med barn", body: "I studierna som gjorde ber\u00f6m k\u00e4nt fick hundratals elever, f\u00f6r det mesta tidiga ton\u00e5ringar, l\u00f6sa samma ganska sv\u00e5ra icke-verbala IQ-uppgifter och fick sedan ett av tv\u00e5 budskap: ber\u00f6m f\u00f6r f\u00f6rm\u00e5ga, som att de m\u00e5ste vara smarta p\u00e5 detta, eller ber\u00f6m f\u00f6r anstr\u00e4ngning, som att de m\u00e5ste ha jobbat v\u00e4ldigt h\u00e5rt. Grupperna var exakt lika fr\u00e5n b\u00f6rjan, men efter\u00e5t valde nittio procent av de som ber\u00f6mts f\u00f6r anstr\u00e4ngning en utmanande ny uppgift att l\u00e4ra sig av, medan de som ber\u00f6mts f\u00f6r f\u00f6rm\u00e5ga avvisade den, presterade s\u00e4mre n\u00e4r uppgifterna blev sv\u00e5ra och n\u00e4stan fyrtio procent lj\u00f6g om sina resultat. Senare metaanalyser har funnit mindre och oj\u00e4mna effekter f\u00f6r tankes\u00e4ttsinterventioner, och den st\u00f6rsta nationella studien med Dweck som medf\u00f6rfattare visade blygsamma genomsnittliga vinster som fr\u00e4mst fanns hos elever med svagare prestationer, en nyans som \u00e4r \u00e4rlig att h\u00e5lla i minnet bredvid de ursprungliga fynden." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — berömstudierna är inflytelserika men replikationerna har gett blandade resultat."},
            { heading: "Det falska utvecklande tankes\u00e4ttet och modeordets problem", body: "Sedan hennes australiska kollega Susan Mackie ber\u00e4ttat om ett utbrott av ett falskt utvecklande tankes\u00e4tt lade Dweck till ett avsnitt i den uppdaterade utg\u00e5van d\u00e4r hon listar s\u00e4tten id\u00e9n missf\u00f6rstods: \u00f6ppenhet togs f\u00f6r tillv\u00e4xt, tillv\u00e4xt reducerades till ren anstr\u00e4ngning, anstr\u00e4ngning som inte fanns ber\u00f6mdes och anstr\u00e4ngningsber\u00f6m anv\u00e4ndes som tr\u00f6stpris f\u00f6r barn som inte l\u00e4rde sig. Hennes r\u00e4ttelse \u00e4r att processen som ber\u00f6mdes i forskningen alltid var knuten till l\u00e4rande, framsteg och prestation, att det \u00e4r en tom uppmuntran att s\u00e4ga att barn kan allt utan att ge dem f\u00e4rdigheter och resurser, och att alla \u00e4r en blandning av b\u00e5da tankes\u00e4tten, s\u00e5 att st\u00e4mpla sig sj\u00e4lv som allt eller inget \u00e4r att missa po\u00e4ngen." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — författarens kommentar om popularisering; metastödet i grunden är svagt."},
          ]
        }
    }
,
    {
        id: "phase7a-mindset_dive2",
        title: "From Talent Myth to Growth Culture: Mindset in School, Sports, Business, and Love",
        framework_description: "A mindset is not a personality type. It is a belief about whether your basic qualities, such as your intelligence, your personality, or your talent, can be developed, and the same person can hold different mindsets in different domains and situations. Because the belief shapes how people interpret challenge, effort, failure, and feedback, it shows up everywhere: in how parents praise, in how athletes read a loss, in how companies treat talent, and in what partners want from each other. This dive follows the research into those four arenas and then asks what it takes to change a mindset, with an honest look at what the replication debate does and does not undermine.\n.1. School and Parenthood: Feedback That Builds or Breaks\nThe starting point is the two-year study of the junior high transition: the students entered with the same grades and achievement scores, but only the fixed-mindset students declined while growth-mindset students improved. The praise experiments explain why. Praising ability, with words like 'you must be smart at this', pushed students straight into the fixed mindset: after difficulty their performance plummeted, and almost 40 percent of them lied about their scores. Praising process, effort, and strategies had the opposite effect, and 90 percent of effort-praised students chose a challenging new task they could learn from. In the German classrooms studied by Falko Rheinberg, the high- and low-ability groups both ended the year way up high when the teacher believed all children could develop, while the gap stayed intact under fixed-minded teachers. The parenting translation is simple: give children challenging tasks, praise the process rather than the person, and use the word 'yet', because the graduate students who felt like imposters had simply forgotten that they did not yet know how to do research.\n.2. Arenas of Talent: Sports, Business, and Love\nSports is where the belief in the natural runs deepest, and Caster Semenya shows how far observers will go to find one. When the eighteen-year-old South African won the 800 metres at the 2009 world championships, much of the reaction explained her victory through her body and her genes rather than through training and effort, and Dweck uses the case to show how quickly success is reattributed to nature. Her research on champions points the other way: people with a growth mindset find success in doing their best and improving, treat setbacks as a wake-up call, and take charge of the process, as Michael Jordan did when he practiced before school after being cut from his high school team. Business runs on the same talent myth, and an organization can hold a mindset of its own. Dweck's surveys found that employees in cultures of development report more trust, ownership, support for risk-taking, and innovation, while employees in cultures of genius report more cheating, shortcuts, hidden information, and a wish to leave, which is why companies that worship talent end up asking where their talent went. In relationships the question is what an ideal partner is for: fixed-minded people want someone who puts them on a pedestal and worships them, while growth-minded people want someone who sees their faults and helps them work on them, and when the two meet, one person's growth can be the other person's nightmare.\n.3. Change, Honesty, and the Replication Debate\nA mindset can change, and Dweck's journey to a true growth mindset has four steps: embrace the fact that everyone carries some fixed mindset, learn what triggers it, give the fixed-mindset persona a name, and educate it, asking it to come along rather than banning it. The false growth mindset is the trap on the way, using growth talk while keeping fixed judgments, which can backfire, because real change moves people from a judge-and-be-judged framework to a learn-and-help-learn framework that takes time and maintenance. The replication debate asks for honesty here: meta-analyses find small average effects, and some classic results have not always replicated, but the large preregistered national study of more than twelve thousand ninth graders found small, real gains in grades that were strongest among lower-achieving students. What the debate does not undermine is the basic descriptive finding, replicated across labs, that the two beliefs exist, are separate from global self-esteem, and predict how people interpret failure, seek challenge, and use feedback.",
        svg: "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 820 300\"><rect width=\"820\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#181825\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"410\" y=\"30\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#f0f0f6\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fixed vs Growth: The Same Situation, Two Readings</text><text x=\"410\" y=\"49\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#9aa0a6\" text-anchor=\"middle\">How everyday life is interpreted under each mindset</text><rect x=\"30\" y=\"62\" width=\"360\" height=\"26\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#3a2026\"/><text x=\"210\" y=\"79\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#f2c0c8\" text-anchor=\"middle\">FIXED MINDSET</text><rect x=\"430\" y=\"62\" width=\"360\" height=\"26\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#1d3445\"/><text x=\"610\" y=\"79\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#a8d4f2\" text-anchor=\"middle\">GROWTH MINDSET</text><rect x=\"408\" y=\"62\" width=\"3\" height=\"188\" fill=\"#2c2c3c\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"124\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b93a7\">CHALLENGE</text><rect x=\"160\" y=\"102\" width=\"230\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#24182a\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"124\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#d8cbd3\">A risk of being exposed</text><rect x=\"430\" y=\"102\" width=\"360\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#152631\"/><text x=\"445\" y=\"124\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#c7ddef\">A chance to grow</text><text x=\"35\" y=\"162\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b93a7\">SETBACK</text><rect x=\"160\" y=\"140\" width=\"230\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#24182a\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"162\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#d8cbd3\">Proof I am a failure</text><rect x=\"430\" y=\"140\" width=\"360\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#152631\"/><text x=\"445\" y=\"162\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#c7ddef\">Information to act on</text><text x=\"35\" y=\"200\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b93a7\">EFFORT</text><rect x=\"160\" y=\"178\" width=\"230\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#24182a\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"200\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#d8cbd3\">A sign I lack talent</text><rect x=\"430\" y=\"178\" width=\"360\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#152631\"/><text x=\"445\" y=\"200\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#c7ddef\">What ignites ability</text><text x=\"35\" y=\"238\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b93a7\">ASKING FOR HELP</text><rect x=\"160\" y=\"216\" width=\"230\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#24182a\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"238\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#d8cbd3\">An admission of weakness</text><rect x=\"430\" y=\"216\" width=\"360\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#152631\"/><text x=\"445\" y=\"238\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"#c7ddef\">A tool for learning</text><rect x=\"30\" y=\"262\" width=\"760\" height=\"28\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#22222e\"/><text x=\"410\" y=\"280\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#aab3c5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Change is a journey: embrace it, learn the triggers, name the persona, educate it</text></svg>",
        key_takeaways: [
          "A mindset is a belief about whether basic qualities can change, not a personality type, and the same person can hold different mindsets for intelligence, personality, and talent in different situations.",
          "Process praise and challenging tasks build learning: in Dweck's experiments, 90 percent of effort-praised students chose a challenging task, while ability praise pushed students into avoiding challenges and hiding poor scores.",
          "In sports and business, the talent myth misleads: observers and organizations that worship innate gifts produce fear, cheating, and groupthink, while cultures of development report more trust, innovation, and honest risk-taking.",
          "Growth-minded people want partners who foster their development rather than pedestals that worship them, and mindset change is a real but gradual journey whose effects, the replication evidence shows, are modest and strongest among at-risk groups.",
        ],
        sections: [
          { heading: "Mindset: The New Psychology of Success", source: "Dweck, C. S. (2006)", body: "The book assembles decades of studies showing that beliefs about the malleability of intelligence, personality, and talent predict how people react to challenge, failure, and feedback in school, sports, business, and relationships. The evidence runs from the two-year junior high transition study and the praise experiments to brain-wave research, in which only growth-minded students paid close attention to information that could help them learn. Dweck is careful to define the growth mindset as a belief cultivated through effort, strategies, and help from others, not as cheerleading or effort praise with no learning attached." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — popular book; meta-analytic effects of mindset interventions are small."},
          { heading: "The Meta-Analytic Evidence", source: "Sisk, V. F. (2018)", body: "These two companion meta-analyses pooled the available research and found a small overall correlation between growth mindset and academic achievement, together with small average effects of mindset interventions. The effects grew larger among at-risk and struggling students, which fits a pattern of conditional rather than universal effects. The authors called for more rigorous research, and the preregistered national experiment by David Yeager and colleagues, with more than twelve thousand ninth graders, subsequently found small but genuine grade gains concentrated among lower-achieving students." , evidence: "Empirical — meta-analysis of mindset effects on academic achievement (Sisk et al. 2018)."},
        ],
        sv: {
          title: "Fr\u00e5n talangmyt till utvecklingskultur: mindset i skola, idrott, arbetsliv och k\u00e4rlek",
          framework_description: "Ett mindset \u00e4r ingen personlighetstyp. Det \u00e4r en f\u00f6rest\u00e4llning om huruvida dina grundl\u00e4ggande egenskaper, som intelligens, personlighet eller talang, kan utvecklas, och samma person kan ha olika mindset inom olika omr\u00e5den och i olika situationer. Eftersom f\u00f6rest\u00e4llningen formar hur m\u00e4nniskor tolkar utmaningar, anstr\u00e4ngning, motg\u00e5ngar och feedback syns den \u00f6verallt: i hur f\u00f6r\u00e4ldrar ber\u00f6mmer, i hur idrottare l\u00e4ser en f\u00f6rlust, i hur f\u00f6retag behandlar talang och i vad partners vill ha av varandra. Den h\u00e4r f\u00f6rdjupningen f\u00f6ljer forskningen in i de fyra arenorna och fr\u00e5gar sedan vad som kr\u00e4vs f\u00f6r att f\u00f6r\u00e4ndra ett mindset, med en \u00e4rlig genomg\u00e5ng av vad replikationsdebatten undergr\u00e4ver och inte undergr\u00e4ver.\n.1. Skola och f\u00f6r\u00e4ldraskap: ber\u00f6m som bygger eller bryter\nUtg\u00e5ngspunkten \u00e4r tv\u00e5\u00e5rsstudien av \u00f6verg\u00e5ngen till h\u00f6gstadiet: eleverna b\u00f6rjade med samma betyg och provresultat, men bara elever med ett fixed mindset tappade i betyg medan elever med growth mindset f\u00f6rb\u00e4ttrades. Ber\u00f6mexperimenten f\u00f6rklarar varf\u00f6r. Att ber\u00f6mma f\u00f6rm\u00e5ga, med ord som du m\u00e5ste vara duktig p\u00e5 det h\u00e4r, sk\u00f6t eleverna rakt in i ett fixed mindset: n\u00e4r uppgifterna blev sv\u00e5ra sj\u00f6nk deras prestationer, och n\u00e4stan fyrtio procent av dem lj\u00f6g om sina resultat. Att ber\u00f6mma processen, anstr\u00e4ngningen och strategierna gav motsatt effekt, och nittio procent av de elever som fick anstr\u00e4ngningsber\u00f6m valde en utmanande ny uppgift som de kunde l\u00e4ra sig av. I de tyska klassrum som Falko Rheinberg studerade slutade b\u00e5de den h\u00f6ga och den l\u00e5ga gruppen \u00e5ret i topp hos l\u00e4rare som trodde att alla barn kan utvecklas, medan klyftan stod kvar hos l\u00e4rare med fixed mindset. L\u00e4rdomen f\u00f6r f\u00f6r\u00e4ldrar \u00e4r enkel: ge barnen utmanande uppgifter, ber\u00f6m processen snarare \u00e4n personen och anv\u00e4nd ordet \u00e4nnu, f\u00f6r forskarstudenterna som k\u00e4nde sig som bluffmakare hade helt enkelt gl\u00f6mt att de \u00e4nnu inte kunde g\u00f6ra forskning.\n.2. Talangens arenor: idrott, arbetsliv och k\u00e4rlek\nInom idrotten sitter tron p\u00e5 naturbeg\u00e5vningen djupast, och Caster Semenya visar hur l\u00e5ngt betraktare g\u00e5r f\u00f6r att hitta en. N\u00e4r den arton\u00e5riga sydafrikanskan vann 800 meter vid VM 2009 f\u00f6rklarades segern i stor utstr\u00e4ckning med hennes kropp och hennes gener snarare \u00e4n med tr\u00e4ning och anstr\u00e4ngning, och Dweck anv\u00e4nder fallet f\u00f6r att visa hur snabbt framg\u00e5ng tillskrivs naturen. Hennes forskning om m\u00e4stare pekar \u00e5t andra h\u00e5llet: personer med growth mindset finner framg\u00e5ng i att g\u00f6ra sitt b\u00e4sta och f\u00f6rb\u00e4ttras, behandlar motg\u00e5ngar som en v\u00e4ckarklocka och tar kommandot \u00f6ver processen, som Michael Jordan som tr\u00e4nade f\u00f6re skolan efter att ha petats fr\u00e5n skollaget. Arbetslivet lever p\u00e5 samma talangmyt, och en hel organisation kan ha ett eget mindset. Dwecks enk\u00e4ter visade att medarbetare i utvecklingskulturer rapporterar mer f\u00f6rtroende, delaktighet, st\u00f6d f\u00f6r risktagande och nyskapande, medan medarbetare i genikulturer rapporterar mer fusk, genv\u00e4gar, hemlighetsmakeri och en \u00f6nskan att sluta, vilket \u00e4r varf\u00f6r f\u00f6retag som dyrkar talang till slut fr\u00e5gar vart talangen tog v\u00e4gen. I k\u00e4rlek handlar fr\u00e5gan om vad en ideal partner \u00e4r till f\u00f6r: personer med fixed mindset vill ha n\u00e5gon som st\u00e4ller dem p\u00e5 en piedestal och dyrkar dem, medan personer med growth mindset vill ha n\u00e5gon som ser deras brister och hj\u00e4lper dem att arbeta med dem, och n\u00e4r de tv\u00e5 m\u00f6ts kan den enes v\u00e4xt bli den andres mardr\u00f6m.\n.3. F\u00f6r\u00e4ndringens mekanik och den \u00e4rliga vetenskapen\nEtt mindset kan f\u00f6r\u00e4ndras, och Dwecks resa mot ett \u00e4kta growth mindset har fyra steg: omfamna att alla b\u00e4r p\u00e5 ett visst m\u00e5tt av fixed mindset, l\u00e4r dig vad som triggar det, ge den fasta personan ett namn och utbilda den, genom att be den f\u00f6lja med p\u00e5 resan i st\u00e4llet f\u00f6r att f\u00f6rbjuda den. Det falska growth mindsetet \u00e4r f\u00e4llan p\u00e5 v\u00e4gen, att anv\u00e4nda utvecklingens spr\u00e5k men beh\u00e5lla de gamla omd\u00f6mena, vilket kan sl\u00e5 tillbaka, f\u00f6r en verklig f\u00f6r\u00e4ndring r\u00f6r sig fr\u00e5n ett ramverk av att d\u00f6ma och bli d\u00f6md till ett ramverk av att l\u00e4ra och hj\u00e4lpa andra att l\u00e4ra, och den tar tid och underh\u00e5ll. Replikationsdebatten kr\u00e4ver \u00e4rlighet h\u00e4r: metaanalyser hittar sm\u00e5 genomsnittliga effekter, och en del klassiska resultat har inte alltid \u00e5terupprepats, men den stora f\u00f6rhandsregistrerade nationella studien med \u00f6ver tolv tusen niondeklassare fann sm\u00e5 men verkliga vinster i betygen, starkast bland elever med svagare studieresultat. Det debatten inte undergr\u00e4ver \u00e4r den grundl\u00e4ggande deskriptiva uppt\u00e4ckten, upprepad i laboratorier v\u00e4rlden \u00f6ver, att de tv\u00e5 f\u00f6rest\u00e4llningarna finns, \u00e4r \u00e5tskilda fr\u00e5n global sj\u00e4lvk\u00e4nsla och f\u00f6ruts\u00e4ger hur m\u00e4nniskor tolkar motg\u00e5ngar, s\u00f6ker utmaningar och anv\u00e4nder feedback.",
          key_takeaways: [
            "Ett mindset \u00e4r en f\u00f6rest\u00e4llning om huruvida grundl\u00e4ggande egenskaper kan f\u00f6r\u00e4ndras, inte en personlighetstyp, och samma person kan ha olika mindset f\u00f6r intelligens, personlighet och talang i olika situationer.",
            "Processber\u00f6m och utmanande uppgifter bygger l\u00e4rande: i Dwecks experiment valde nittio procent av de elever som fick anstr\u00e4ngningsber\u00f6m en utmanande uppgift, medan f\u00f6rm\u00e5gsber\u00f6m fick elever att undvika utmaningar och d\u00f6lja d\u00e5liga resultat.",
            "I idrott och arbetsliv vilseleder talangmyten: betraktare och organisationer som dyrkar medf\u00f6dda g\u00e5vor skapar r\u00e4dsla, fusk och gruppt\u00e4nkande, medan utvecklingskulturer rapporterar mer f\u00f6rtroende, nyskapande och \u00e4rligt risktagande.",
            "Personer med growth mindset vill ha partners som fr\u00e4mjar deras utveckling snarare \u00e4n piedestaler som dyrkar dem, och f\u00f6r\u00e4ndring av mindset \u00e4r en verklig men l\u00e5ngsam resa vars effekter, enligt replikationsforskningen, \u00e4r m\u00e5ttliga och starkast bland grupper i riskzonen.",
          ],
          sections: [
            { heading: "Grundboken Mindset", body: "Boken samlar decennier av studier som visar att f\u00f6rest\u00e4llningar om intelligensens, personlighetens och talangens f\u00f6r\u00e4nderlighet f\u00f6ruts\u00e4ger hur m\u00e4nniskor reagerar p\u00e5 utmaningar, motg\u00e5ngar och feedback i skola, idrott, arbetsliv och relationer. Bevisen str\u00e4cker sig fr\u00e5n tv\u00e5\u00e5rsstudien av h\u00f6gstadie\u00f6verg\u00e5ngen och ber\u00f6mexperimenten till hj\u00e4rnv\u00e5gsforskning, d\u00e4r bara elever med growth mindset \u00e4gnade noggrann uppm\u00e4rksamhet \u00e5t information som kunde hj\u00e4lpa dem att l\u00e4ra. Dweck \u00e4r noga med att definiera growth mindset som en f\u00f6rest\u00e4llning som odlas genom anstr\u00e4ngning, strategier och hj\u00e4lp fr\u00e5n andra, inte som hejarop eller anstr\u00e4ngningsber\u00f6m utan l\u00e4rande." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — populärvetenskaplig bok; metaanalytiska effekter av tankesättsinterventioner är små."},
            { heading: "Metaanalyserna", body: "De tv\u00e5 metaanalyserna av Sisk med kollegor slog samman den publicerade forskningen och fann ett litet samband mellan growth mindset och skolresultat, tillsammans med sm\u00e5 genomsnittliga effekter av mindsetinterventioner. Effekterna blev st\u00f6rre bland elever i riskzonen och elever som k\u00e4mpade, vilket passar ett m\u00f6nster av villkorade snarare \u00e4n generella effekter. F\u00f6rfattarna efterlyste mer rigor\u00f6s forskning, och det f\u00f6rhandsregistrerade nationella f\u00f6rs\u00f6ket av David Yeager med kollegor, med \u00f6ver tolv tusen niondeklassare, fann d\u00e4refter sm\u00e5 men verkliga betygsvinster koncentrerade till elever med svagare studieresultat." , evidence: "Empirical — metaanalys av tankesättets effekter på skolprestation (Sisk m.fl. 2018)."},
          ]
        }
    }
