    {
        id: "phase7a-drive_dive1",
        title: "The Third Drive: Why Intrinsic Motivation Beats Carrots and Sticks",
        framework_description: "Drive is Daniel Pink's popular account of self-determination theory, the science of why we do what we do. Pink argues that the carrot-and-stick logic of Motivation 2.0, reward the good and punish the bad, no longer fits the way we actually work. This dive covers the evidence for a third drive, the pleasure of the activity itself, and how autonomy, mastery, and purpose can redesign tasks, workplaces, and education.\n.1. Intrinsic Motivation and the Overjustification Effect\nIn 1949 Harry Harlow found that rhesus monkeys solved mechanical puzzles with no food, water, or applause, because the puzzle itself provided intrinsic reward. When Harlow later added raisins, the monkeys made more errors, and Edward Deci found the same pattern with money: paid Soma puzzle solvers lost interest once the pay stopped. Promising a preschooler a certificate for drawing can make the child draw and then lose further interest in drawing, the effect called overjustification.\n.2. Autonomy, Mastery, and Purpose: The Innate Needs\nSelf-determination theory begins with universal human needs: competence, autonomy, and relatedness, and when they are satisfied we are motivated, productive, and happy. Pink condenses them into the three elements of Type I behavior: autonomy, the desire to direct our own lives; mastery, the urge to get better at something that matters; and purpose, the yearning to serve something larger than ourselves. These are the nutrients that keep intrinsic motivation alive, and they explain why people built Wikipedia for free.\n.3. Rewards Narrow Focus: The Candle Problem and the Redesign of Work\nIn Glucksberg's version of the candle problem, the group racing for a prize solved the puzzle nearly three and a half minutes slower than the reward-free group: rewards narrow focus, which helps routine tasks but blunts the wide view that heuristic work demands. That is why Pink proposes redesigning tasks, workplaces, and education around self-direction, from Goldilocks tasks that match challenge to ability to the results-only work environment, where people are judged on outcomes, not hours. In schools, autonomy means letting students choose projects and set their own goals instead of bribing them with pizza, cash, and iPods.",
        svg: "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 820 300\"><rect width=\"820\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#181825\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"410\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#cdd6f4\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Third Drive</text><text x=\"410\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#89b4fa\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"14\">Intrinsic motivation beats if-then rewards on creative work</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"85\" width=\"350\" height=\"185\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1e1e2e\"/><text x=\"215\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f38ba8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\">Motivation 2.0</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">If-then rewards</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">Narrow focus</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"208\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">Fails the candle problem</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"238\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">Undermines interest</text><rect x=\"430\" y=\"85\" width=\"350\" height=\"185\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1e1e2e\"/><text x=\"605\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6e3a1\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\">Motivation 3.0</text><text x=\"605\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">Autonomy</text><text x=\"605\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">Mastery</text><text x=\"605\" y=\"208\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">Purpose</text><text x=\"605\" y=\"238\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#a6adc8\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\">Judged on results, not hours</text><text x=\"410\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94e2d5\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"14\">From the candle problem to ROWE: self-direction carries the third drive</text></svg>",
        key_takeaways: [
          "Intrinsic motivation is the third drive: doing something for its own sake, and contingent if-then rewards can undermine it.",
          "Extrinsic rewards and punishments work for routine tasks but narrow focus and can devastate heuristic, creative work.",
          "Autonomy, mastery, and purpose are the three innate needs that sustain intrinsic motivation over the long run.",
          "Redesigning tasks, workplaces such as ROWE, and schools around self-direction protects lasting engagement.",
        ],
        sections: [
          { heading: "From Harlow's Monkeys to the Candle Problem", source: "Pink, D. H. (2009)", body: "In 1949 Harry Harlow found that rhesus monkeys solved mechanical puzzles with no food reward, and Edward Deci later showed that paying students for Soma puzzles made them lose interest once the money stopped. Contingent rewards can drain the joy from an activity, converting play into work. Glucksberg's candle problem then showed that money even slowed people down on a task that demanded a wide, inventive view." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — popular synthesis; Harlow's experiments are real but the broader claims are simplified."},
          { heading: "Redesigning Work and School Around the Third Drive", source: "Deci, E. L., & Ryan, R. M. (2000)", body: "Self-determination theory names competence, autonomy, and relatedness as innate psychological needs, which Pink translates into the autonomy, mastery, and purpose that sustain Type I motivation. In a results-only work environment people are judged on outcomes rather than hours, and Goldilocks tasks match challenge to ability so work produces flow. Schools can preserve intrinsic interest by letting students choose projects and set their own goals instead of paying them for performance." , evidence: "Empirical — self-determination theory is supported by decades of experimental and field research."},
        ],
        sv: {
          title: "Den tredje drivkraften: inre motivation bortom morot och piska",
          framework_description: "Vad f\u00e5r m\u00e4nniskor att prestera som b\u00e4st? Daniel Pinks bok Drive sammanfattar sj\u00e4lvbest\u00e4mmandeteorin och visar att morot och piska ofta sl\u00e5r fel. N\u00e4r uppgiften \u00e4r tillr\u00e4ckligt kr\u00e4vande styrs vi i st\u00e4llet av en tredje drivkraft: lusten att g\u00f6ra n\u00e5got f\u00f6r dess egen skull.\n.1. Inre och yttre motivation: \u00f6verjustifieringseffekten\nHarry Harlows apor l\u00f6ste mekaniska pussel utan att f\u00e5 vare sig mat eller ber\u00f6m, och Edward Deci visade att studenter som fick betalt f\u00f6r att l\u00e4gga Soma-pusslet tappade intresset n\u00e4r pengarna slutade komma. N\u00e4r en yttre bel\u00f6ning kopplas till n\u00e5got vi redan tycker om kan lusten f\u00f6rsvinna: barn som lovades ett diplom f\u00f6r att teckna ritade mindre efter\u00e5t. Fenomenet kallas \u00f6verjustifieringseffekten och g\u00e4ller villkorade bel\u00f6ningar av typen om du g\u00f6r det h\u00e4r f\u00e5r du det d\u00e4r.\n.2. Autonomi, m\u00e4sterskap och syfte: de medf\u00f6dda behoven\nEnligt Deci och Ryan har m\u00e4nniskan tre medf\u00f6dda psykologiska behov: kompetens, autonomi och samh\u00f6righet. Pink \u00f6vers\u00e4tter dem till tre n\u00e4rings\u00e4mnen f\u00f6r inre motivation: autonomi, lusten att styra \u00f6ver sitt eget liv; m\u00e4sterskap, driften att bli b\u00e4ttre p\u00e5 n\u00e5got som betyder n\u00e5got; och syfte, l\u00e4ngtan att tj\u00e4na n\u00e5got st\u00f6rre \u00e4n sig sj\u00e4lv. N\u00e4r dessa behov tillgodoses blir vi motiverade, produktiva och lyckliga.\n.3. Bel\u00f6ningar smalnar av fokus: ljusstakeproblemet och ombyggda arbetsplatser\nI Glucksbergs variant av ljusstakeproblemet l\u00f6ste de som t\u00e4vlade om pengar uppgiften n\u00e4stan tre och en halv minut l\u00e5ngsammare \u00e4n gruppen utan bel\u00f6ning: bel\u00f6ningar smalnar av fokus och \u00e4r d\u00e4rf\u00f6r skadliga f\u00f6r kreativa, heuristiska uppgifter. D\u00e4rf\u00f6r bygger man om arbetsuppgifter, arbetsplatser och skolor kring sj\u00e4lvstyrning, till exempel genom results-only work environment d\u00e4r medarbetare bed\u00f6ms p\u00e5 resultat i st\u00e4llet f\u00f6r p\u00e5 arbetstid. I skolan inneb\u00e4r det att elever f\u00e5r v\u00e4lja projekt och s\u00e4tta egna m\u00e5l i st\u00e4llet f\u00f6r att lockas med pizza och pengar.",
          key_takeaways: [
            "Inre motivation \u00e4r den tredje drivkraften: att g\u00f6ra n\u00e5got f\u00f6r dess egen skull, och villkorade bel\u00f6ningar kan undergr\u00e4va den.",
            "Yttre bel\u00f6ningar och bestraffningar fungerar f\u00f6r rutinuppgifter men smalnar av fokus och kan skada kreativt arbete.",
            "Autonomi, m\u00e4sterskap och syfte \u00e4r de tre behoven som h\u00e5ller den inre motivationen vid liv.",
            "Att utforma uppgifter, arbetsplatser som ROWE och undervisning kring sj\u00e4lvstyrning skyddar engagemanget p\u00e5 l\u00e5ng sikt.",
          ],
          sections: [
            { heading: "Fr\u00e5n Harlows apor till ljusstakeproblemet", body: "Harlow uppt\u00e4ckte att apor l\u00f6ste pussel utan bel\u00f6ning, och Deci visade att betalning kan f\u00e5 oss att tappa intresset f\u00f6r sj\u00e4lva aktiviteten. Villkorade bel\u00f6ningar, om du g\u00f6r det h\u00e4r f\u00e5r du det d\u00e4r, f\u00f6rvandlar lek till arbete och g\u00f6r att lusten f\u00f6rsvinner. Glucksbergs ljusstakeproblem visade att pengar till och med gjorde kreativt t\u00e4nkande l\u00e5ngsammare." , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — populärvetenskaplig syntes; Harlows experiment är äkta men de bredare anspråken är förenklade."},
            { heading: "Arbete och skola byggs om kring den tredje drivkraften", body: "Autonomi handlar om frihet \u00f6ver uppgift, tid, teknik och team, och results-only work environment l\u00e5ter medarbetare styra \u00f6ver n\u00e4r och hur de arbetar, s\u00e5 l\u00e4nge resultaten levereras. Uppgifter av lagom sv\u00e5righetsgrad ger flyt n\u00e4r utmaningen matchar f\u00f6rm\u00e5gan. I skolan g\u00e4ller det att l\u00e5ta elever v\u00e4lja projekt och s\u00e4tta egna m\u00e5l i st\u00e4llet f\u00f6r att betala dem f\u00f6r att l\u00e4sa b\u00f6cker." , evidence: "Empirical — självbestämmandeteorin stöds av decennier av experimentell och fältbaserad forskning."},
          ]
        }
    }
