    {
      id: "concept-self-presentation",
      title: "Strategic Self-Presentation",
      framework_description: "Impression management is the goal-directed process of controlling how others perceive us. Rooted in Erving Goffman\u2019s dramaturgical metaphor, it views social interaction as a performance where individuals strategically present themselves to create desired impressions.\n.1. Goffman\u2019s Dramaturgical Framework\nErving Goffman (1959) proposed that social life is like theater. Individuals are performers on a front stage (public interactions) who manage settings, appearance, and manner to convey desired impressions. Backstage (private moments) allows relaxation from performance demands. Interaction rituals and face-work maintain social order.\n.2. The Five Self-Presentation Tactics\nJones and Pittman (1982) identified five strategic self-presentation tactics: ingratiation (being likeable through flattery and favors), self-promotion (demonstrating competence), exemplification (displaying integrity and moral worth), intimidation (projecting power and danger), and supplication (portraying weakness to elicit help). Each tactic is designed to achieve a specific social goal.\n.3. Self-Monitoring and Individual Differences\nMark Snyder\u2019s self-monitoring construct captures individual differences in impression management. High self-monitors are social chameleons who carefully read situations and adjust their behavior accordingly. Low self-monitors prioritize consistency with internal attitudes over situational demands. Both styles have adaptive advantages and vulnerabilities.",
      links: ["The Laws of Human Nature (Robert Greene).pdf|The Laws of Human Nature - Greene", "Social Intelligence (Daniel Goleman).pdf|Social Intelligence - Goleman"],
      key_takeaways: [
        "Goffman\u2019s dramaturgical metaphor describes social interaction as a managed performance with front stage and backstage regions",
        "Five self-presentation tactics (ingratiation, self-promotion, exemplification, intimidation, supplication) serve different strategic goals",
        "Self-monitoring captures stable individual differences in how people manage their public images",
        "Self-presentation is not inherently deceptive \u2014 it is a fundamental and necessary aspect of social life"
      ],
      sections: [
        { heading: "Facework Across Cultures", source: "Ting-Toomey, S. (1988)", body: "<p>Facework \u2014 the communicative strategies used to maintain, protect, or restore face (social image) \u2014 varies significantly across cultures. In collectivistic cultures (China, Japan, Korea), face is primarily other-oriented: saving others\u2019 face takes precedence over self-enhancement. In individualistic cultures, self-face concerns dominate.</p><p>Cross-cultural facework failures are a common source of miscommunication in intercultural interactions. Understanding whether a culture emphasizes self-face, other-face, or mutual-face concerns is essential for effective impression management across cultural boundaries.</p>" , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — facework theory is widely used in applied communication research but rests largely on qualitative work."},
        { heading: "Digital Impression Management", source: "Walther, J.B. (2007)", body: "<p>Online environments create unique opportunities for impression management. Users can selectively self-present through profile photos, status updates, and content sharing. Hyperpersonal communication theory suggests that online interactions can sometimes exceed face-to-face communication in intimacy because users have more time to craft messages.</p><p>However, digital footprints create permanent records that can contradict self-presentation efforts. The concept of context collapse \u2014 when multiple audiences converge in the same social media space \u2014 complicates impression management as users must address diverse audiences simultaneously.</p>" , evidence: "Empirical — hyperpersonal model is supported by experimental online-communication studies."}
      ],
      sv: {
        title: "Strategisk självpresentation",
        framework_description: "Impression management förklarar hur människor kontrollerar andras uppfattningar.",
        key_takeaways: ["Goffmans dramaturgiska metafor", "Fem taktiker tjänar olika mål"],
        sections: [{ heading: "Facework över kulturer", body: "<p>Facework varierar mellan kulturer.</p>" , evidence: "Observational–Tactical — facework-teorin är vitt använd i tillämpad kommunikationsforskning men vilar främst på kvalitativa studier."}]
      }
    }
